            workedExample: 'A student measures knee flexion during walking in three teenage athletes and plots it against a widely reproduced "normal" band from a children\'s clinical dataset (Helen Hayes marker set, collected at a slow comfortable clinical speed). Two of their subjects fall "outside normal." Before concluding anything is wrong, the student checks the reference: it was collected on younger children at a slower speed with a different marker model. They correctly reframe the finding — the deviation may reflect the SPEED and AGE mismatch and a different processing pipeline, not a real gait abnormality — and note in their limitations that a valid comparison would need an age-, speed-, and protocol-matched norm.',
            judgeFraming: 'Judges probe "how do you know that\'s abnormal?" because comparing to a normative band is the most common place students over-trust a number. Naming real reference datasets (Kadaba/Õunpuu Helen Hayes data, Winter\'s norms) AND the three things that break their transfer — age, walking speed, and marker/processing protocol — shows you treat "normal" as a context-bound measurement, not a universal law. That distinction is exactly what a judge uses to tell a careful analyst from someone who just reads whether a line is inside or outside a shaded band.',
