            id: 'gaitcore-clinical-performance-translation-primary-vs-compensation-gage',
            label: 'Primary Deviation vs. Compensation and the Newington/Gage Legacy',
            readingMinutes: 6,
            sections: [
              {
                heading: 'James Gage and the discipline of asking "why"',
                body: 'James R. Gage, an orthopedic surgeon who led the gait laboratories at Newington Children\'s Hospital and then at Gillette Children\'s in St. Paul, is the figure most associated with turning gait analysis into a clinical discipline. His books — including "Gait Analysis in Cerebral Palsy" (1991) and the later "The Identification and Treatment of Gait Problems in Cerebral Palsy" — codified a way of thinking that the field still teaches: never treat a deviation until you know whether it is PRIMARY (the direct result of the neurological or musculoskeletal impairment), a COMPENSATION (a strategy the rest of the body adopts to keep walking around the primary problem), or plain measurement noise. Gage described the prerequisites of normal gait — stability in stance, foot clearance in swing, appropriate pre-positioning of the foot for contact, adequate step length, and energy conservation — and read pathological gait as the body\'s best attempt to preserve those goals despite an impairment.',
              },
              {
                heading: 'Three causes, one deviation on the graph',
                body: 'The Gage framework insists that the SAME curve can arise from different causes with opposite treatments. Take a stiff, extended knee in swing. It could be a primary rectus femoris spasticity (the muscle firing when it should be silent, mechanically preventing knee flexion) — treated by transferring or lengthening rectus femoris. It could be a compensation: the knee is held stiff and the leg circumducted or vaulted to clear a foot that cannot dorsiflex, in which case the ankle is the primary problem and touching the knee would remove a needed strategy. Or the excess could be an artifact of a mislabeled marker or a cross-talk between planes. Distinguishing these requires the full data set — dynamic EMG to catch out-of-phase rectus activity, kinematics at the ankle to see whether clearance is the real driver, and a passive exam to separate a fixed contracture from a dynamic (correctable) one.',
              },
              {
                heading: 'The cost of chasing a compensation',
                body: 'Operating on a compensation is not merely wasted — it can be harmful. If a child vaults on their toes to clear a dropped contralateral foot, and a surgeon "corrects" the vaulting by weakening the calf, the child loses the clearance strategy and trips. Gage\'s legacy is the demand for a written argument, per deviation, classifying it as primary or compensatory and justifying it from the objective record before any procedure is chosen. This is also why single-event multilevel surgery must be planned holistically: fix the primary problem at one level and several compensations elsewhere may resolve on their own, so treating them as independent targets over-operates. The discipline is fundamentally causal — it treats the gait as a system solving a mechanical problem, not a list of independent abnormalities.',
              },
            ],
            workedExample: 'A student re-analyzes a published-style case: a child shows (1) reduced ankle dorsiflexion in swing on the left, (2) increased left hip and knee flexion in swing, and (3) a stiff, extended RIGHT knee through stance. Applying the Gage framework, they argue the left ankle equinus is the PRIMARY deviation (it sits at the impaired joint/phase), the exaggerated left hip/knee flexion is a COMPENSATION to clear the dropped foot, and the stiff right stance knee is a further compensation — vaulting on the stance limb to give the swing leg extra clearance time. Their predicted test: restore left ankle dorsiflexion with an ankle-foot orthosis and BOTH compensations (left steppage and right vaulting) should diminish, because both existed only to serve clearance of the left foot.',
            judgeFraming: 'Judges ask you to name which deviation you would treat, and why the others are compensations, because the entire Gage discipline — and the safety of a real surgical plan — rides on that classification. A student who can point to a curve, invoke the prerequisites of normal gait (here, foot clearance in swing), and predict that fixing one primary deviation resolves two compensations is demonstrating the causal, systems view that separates a defensible gait interpretation from a list of abnormal numbers.',
