            sections: [
              {
                heading: 'Why walking speed earned "the sixth vital sign"',
                body: 'Beyond the specialist lab, a single number measured with a stopwatch and a taped 4-meter course has become a population-level health marker: usual walking speed. A widely cited pooled analysis by Stephanie Studenski and colleagues (JAMA, 2011), combining nine cohort studies of nearly 34,000 older adults, showed that gait speed predicted survival — with each 0.1 m/s increment associated with meaningfully better predicted survival, and speeds around 0.8 m/s or below flagging poorer prognosis. Because it integrates the health of so many systems (muscle, heart and lung, balance, cognition, motivation), gait speed is sometimes called the "sixth vital sign." A related, older result — Lundin-Olsson, Nyberg and Gustafson\'s observation (Lancet, 1997) that a person who stops walking when they start talking is at elevated fall risk (the "stops walking when talking" sign) — foreshadowed today\'s dual-task testing.',
              },
              {
                heading: 'Variability: the average hides the risk',
                body: 'A mean can look reassuring while the person is quietly unsafe. Stride-to-stride VARIABILITY — usually reported as the coefficient of variation of stride time or stride length — reflects the consistency of motor control, and higher variability predicts falls even when average speed and step length look normal. The intuition: an occasional badly-timed or badly-placed step is what actually causes a fall, and only variability captures those tail events; the average smooths them away. This is why fall-risk gait studies report variability alongside means, and why a finding of INCREASED variability can be the headline result of a study even when the averages are unremarkable.',
              },
              {
                heading: 'Standardized bedside tests a student can run',
                body: 'Several validated, near-equipment-free tests turn gait into a risk number. The Timed Up and Go (Podsiadlo and Richardson, 1991) times a rise-walk-3m-turn-return-sit sequence, with longer times flagging elevated fall risk. A fixed-distance gait-speed test (e.g. 4-meter walk) yields the single powerful number from the Studenski analysis. Dual-task tests — walking while counting backward by threes or naming animals — unmask people whose gait deteriorates sharply when attention is divided, exposing thin cognitive reserve. All three need only a stopwatch and a tape measure, which is exactly what makes gait-as-vital-sign an ethically clean, high-value target for a student project on healthy adult volunteers of differing age or fitness.',
              },
            ],
            workedExample: 'A student measures usual gait speed over a 4-meter course, a Timed Up and Go, and stride-time variability (from 30 seconds of steady walking timed against a metronome-free footfall count) in two groups of volunteers: young adults and their grandparents. The young group averages 1.4 m/s with a stride-time coefficient of variation near 2%; the older group averages a still-respectable 1.1 m/s but with a coefficient of variation near 5% and one grandparent showing 8%. The student reports that the speeds alone would have understated the difference, and that the elevated variability — the tail of inconsistent steps — is the more sensitive signal, citing the reasoning that a single mistimed step, not the average, is what precipitates a fall.',
            judgeFraming: 'Judges ask "why report variability and not just average speed?" because the naive choice — a single mean — is exactly what makes fragile walkers look fine, and a study that reports only means has missed the population it should worry about. A student who measures variability or a dual-task decrement, explains that falls come from tail-event mis-steps the average erases, and can cite gait speed\'s standing as a "sixth vital sign," shows the interpretive sophistication that turns a stopwatch exercise into a genuine risk-prediction study.',
