                      b: 'Correct — rotational in-toeing commonly reflects a fixed bony torsion (femoral anteversion or internal tibial torsion); because the deformity is skeletal, muscle strengthening cannot correct the alignment.',
                      c: 'An antalgic, pain-driven pattern shows shortened stance time and guarding, not a fixed rotational in-toe unresponsive to strengthening.',
