              sections: [
                {
                  heading: 'Foot-drop: a lower-motor-neuron clearance failure',
                  body: 'Foot-drop is the failure to dorsiflex the ankle during SWING, so the toes hang down and threaten to catch the ground as the limb advances. Its classic cause is weakness or paralysis of the ankle dorsiflexors — tibialis anterior and the toe extensors — usually from a lesion of the common peroneal (fibular) nerve or the L4–L5 roots. It is a LOWER-motor-neuron picture: the affected muscles are weak and often wasted, tone is normal or reduced (flaccid), and reflexes are normal or diminished — a useful contrast with the spastic, hyperreflexic upper-motor-neuron gaits of later lessons. Because the dorsiflexors also eccentrically LOWER the foot after heel strike, foot-drop shows a "foot slap" — an uncontrolled flat-foot or forefoot contact — even when swing clearance is somehow managed.',
                },
                {
                  heading: 'Steppage and circumduction: two ways to buy clearance',
                  body: 'A dangling foot in swing must be lifted somehow, or the toe drags. The two named compensations are STEPPAGE — exaggerated hip and knee flexion to hoist the whole foot high, like stepping over an invisible obstacle — and hip-hike/CIRCUMDUCTION, swinging the leg out in an arc so the toe skims past. On the kinematic curves, steppage appears as increased peak hip and knee flexion in swing beyond the normal band; circumduction appears in the frontal/transverse planes as an abducted, arcing hip path. Both are compensations, not the primary lesion — a distinction the branch keeps returning to: the primary problem is a swing-phase dorsiflexion deficit; the high-stepping or arcing is the body’s workaround.',
                },
                {
                  heading: 'From graph to device: AFOs and functional electrical stimulation',
                  body: 'Foot-drop is one of the most treatable gait deviations. A hinged or posterior-leaf ankle-foot orthosis (AFO) mechanically holds the ankle at neutral so the toe clears without high-stepping and lands controlled. Alternatively, functional electrical stimulation (FES) of the common peroneal nerve, timed to swing, contracts the dorsiflexors on cue — the approach popularized by Liberson and colleagues in 1961, one of the first clinical uses of FES. Randomized and cohort trials (for example in post-stroke drop-foot) have generally found FES and AFOs comparably effective for walking speed, with some evidence of a "carry-over" training effect from FES; the point for a gait study is that the same swing-phase clearance failure, read off the ankle and knee curves, motivates the device choice.',
                },
              ],
              workedExample: 'A student records a subject who cannot lift the toes in swing, high-steps with exaggerated hip and knee flexion, and slaps the foot flat at contact, with weak tibialis anterior and normal reflexes. The student reads foot-drop (a lower-motor-neuron swing-phase dorsiflexor failure) with a steppage compensation, and supports it on the graphs by pointing to a plantarflexed swing ankle plus above-normal swing hip/knee flexion peaks. They then note that a posterior-leaf AFO or peroneal-nerve FES timed to swing would restore clearance — tying the intervention directly to the phase and mechanism of the deviation rather than to the surface label "walks funny."',
              judgeFraming: 'Judges push on foot-drop because it is the cleanest example of separating a PRIMARY deficit (swing dorsiflexion failure) from a COMPENSATION (steppage or circumduction) — and because it is easy to confuse with the spastic equinus of the previous lesson despite the opposite muscle failure. A student who names the lower-motor-neuron mechanism, points to the swing-phase clearance strategy on the correct curves, and links it to an AFO/FES rationale shows the layered "primary versus compensatory" reasoning this branch is built to reward.',
