              sections: [
                {
                  heading: 'A basal-ganglia disorder of movement scaling',
                  body: 'Parkinson’s disease degrades the dopaminergic nigrostriatal pathway, impairing the basal ganglia’s role in SCALING and automating movement. The gait signature follows: short, shuffling steps with reduced step length and reduced foot clearance, a stooped forward (flexed) posture, and reduced or absent arm swing — often ASYMMETRIC early in disease, tracking the more-affected body side. Reduced arm swing is one of the earliest and most sensitive signs. On the spatiotemporal measures the picture is reduced step length with a compensatory elevated cadence (many small steps), reduced ankle push-off power, and — importantly — increased stride-to-stride variability. Unlike cerebellar ataxia, the base of support is typically NARROW, not wide.',
                },
                {
                  heading: 'Festination and freezing of gait',
                  body: 'Two hallmark phenomena stand out. FESTINATION is a progressive shortening AND quickening of steps: as the stooped posture puts the center of mass ahead of the feet, the person takes ever-smaller, faster steps to chase their balance, appearing to hurry involuntarily. FREEZING OF GAIT (FOG) is a sudden, brief, episodic inability to start or continue stepping — "feet glued to the floor" — most often triggered at gait initiation, turning, or passing through doorways and narrow spaces. FOG is a leading cause of falls in Parkinson’s and is notoriously levodopa-resistant in some patients. Crucially, both festination and freezing are EPISODIC/dynamic phenomena, which is why they can be missed on a single steady-state walk and are probed with turns and dual-tasking.',
                },
                {
                  heading: 'Cueing, levodopa, and variability as a fall signal',
                  body: 'A striking clinical fact is that external CUES can transiently bypass the basal-ganglia deficit: rhythmic auditory cues (a metronome), visual floor lines, or a rhythm to step over can dramatically improve step length and reduce freezing — evidence that the motor program is intact but its internal triggering/scaling is impaired. Dopaminergic medication (levodopa) improves the hypokinetic features (step length, speed) but is less reliable for freezing and postural instability, which is why gait and balance can worsen even in treated patients. For a gait study, the most useful quantitative signal beyond the mean is VARIABILITY: elevated stride-to-stride variability (e.g. in stride time or length) is a robust marker of fall risk in Parkinson’s and older adults — a mean step length can look only mildly short while the variability is the real red flag (work associated with Hausdorff and colleagues on stride-time dynamics).',
                },
              ],
