              sections: [
                {
                  heading: 'The line a student may not cross: no diagnosing',
                  body: 'Interpreting a person\'s gait to assign or confirm a medical diagnosis is the practice of medicine — outside a high-school student\'s scope and, in most jurisdictions, unlicensed. A student project may MEASURE and DESCRIBE gait ("this walk shows reduced stance time on the left, consistent with an antalgic pattern") but must never DIAGNOSE ("this person has hip osteoarthritis"). This is not pedantry: a wrong "diagnosis" can frighten a subject, and even a right one delivered by an untrained person can cause harm and liability. The safe verb set is measure, describe, compare, characterize — never diagnose, screen-for-disease, or recommend-treatment for a real individual.',
                },
                {
                  heading: 'Vulnerable populations and physical safety',
                  body: 'Studying IMPAIRED gait raises two distinct safety layers. First, human-subjects protections: informed consent (and assent plus parental consent for minors), voluntary participation, the right to stop, and privacy of any data or video collected. People with clinical conditions are often a VULNERABLE population — children, older adults, patients — for whom the bar is higher and the appropriate answer for most fairs is simply not to recruit them. Second, physical risk: anyone whose balance is impaired can FALL during a walking task. A safe protocol uses a spotter, a guard rail or gait belt, a clear uncluttered path, footwear checks, and rest breaks, and excludes anyone for whom walking trials are genuinely unsafe. Safety planning is not optional garnish; it is a precondition of doing the study at all.',
                },
                {
                  heading: 'Data, images, and IRB/ISEF oversight',
                  body: 'Gait data is often identifiable — a video shows a face, a rare condition can single a person out. Consent must cover what is recorded, how it is stored, who sees it, and how it is de-identified. In the U.S. science-fair world, the International Science and Engineering Fair (ISEF, run by the Society for Science) sets rules for human-participant research: many projects need review by a properly constituted Institutional Review Board (IRB) or Scientific Review Committee BEFORE data collection, and human-participant, informed-consent, and (for minors) parental-permission forms are mandatory. The cardinal rule is prospective review: you get approval first, then collect. A project that collected patient gait data without prior human-subjects review is not just weak — it can be disqualified.',
                },
              ],
              workedExample: 'A student wants to study "how injury changes walking." Their first plan — filming patients at a local physiotherapy clinic and labeling their conditions — is stopped by their teacher on scope grounds: it recruits a vulnerable clinical population, risks diagnosing, and lacks prior review. They redesign to a SIMULATED, within-subject study in consenting healthy adult volunteers (a reversible pebble-in-shoe antalgic simulation), write an informed-consent form covering the video and its storage, plan a spotter and a clear walking lane, and submit the human-participants and consent forms to their fair\'s Scientific Review Committee for approval BEFORE any filming. In their report they describe gait patterns and explicitly state they neither recruited patients nor diagnosed anyone.',
              judgeFraming: 'Judges (and any competent fair review committee) treat human-subjects ethics as a gate, not a bonus: "Did you get review BEFORE collecting? Did anyone consent? Are you diagnosing?" A single wrong answer can disqualify. A student who can state that they only measure and describe (never diagnose), that they avoided or properly protected vulnerable participants with consent and a safety plan, and that they obtained prospective IRB/SRC review has cleared the exact bar that sinks otherwise-clever pathological-gait projects — and shows the judge they understand research ethics as seriously as the biomechanics.',
