                      a: 'During stance, body weight presses the limb INTO the socket, so suspension is least challenged then.',
                      b: 'Correct — in swing the leg hangs free and the weight of the foot and pylon pulls the socket down, so suspension must resist that downward pull.',
                      c: 'Initial contact is high-force but it re-seats the socket; the risk of the socket pulling off is greatest during the preceding swing.',
                      d: 'The swing phase is precisely when the socket is most likely to slip off, so suspension is not phase-neutral.',
