                      b: 'Correct — with no plantarflexors across the ankle, a passive foot can at best store elastic energy on loading and return part of it; it cannot generate the net positive work the intact ankle produces at terminal stance.',
                      c: 'Alignment affects rollover but is not the reason a passive foot lacks active push-off; even perfectly aligned, it has no muscle to do positive work.',
                      d: 'Residual limbs routinely bear load through the socket; load tolerance is not the mechanism that blocks push-off.',
