                  body: "The tempting conclusion from Lessons 1-5 is 'forefoot striking is simply better because it removes the impact transient.' The mature view is subtler: changing foot-strike does not make the load VANISH — it REDISTRIBUTES it to different tissues. A rearfoot strike sends the abrupt collision up through the heel pad, tibia, and knee; the KNEE tends to see higher patellofemoral and loading demands. A forefoot strike removes the heel transient but loads the ankle plantarflexors, the Achilles tendon, and the calf far more, because the ankle must control a longer eccentric 'give.' So the honest framing is a TRADE-OFF in where the mechanical demand lands, not a free lunch — and which trade-off is preferable depends on which tissue is injured or at risk in a given runner.",
