              sections: [
                {
                  heading: 'The midsole is where the mechanics live',
                  body: 'The MIDSOLE is the layer of foam between the insole under your foot and the rubber outsole that touches the ground — it is the single component that most changes the force, cushioning, and energy behavior a shoe imposes on the body. Midsole foams are POLYMER foams: a solid polymer matrix riddled with tiny gas-filled cells, so that under load the cell walls buckle and the trapped gas compresses, letting the whole slab deform many millimeters and re-expand. Two numbers describe a foam: its DENSITY (mass per volume, in g/cm³, set mostly by how much gas was blown into it) and its resulting STIFFNESS (force per unit compression, in N/mm, from Unit 1). A lower-density foam has more, larger gas cells, compresses more easily under a given load, and weighs less per unit volume — which is why the modern foam race is fundamentally a race to get lower density AND better energy return at the same time.',
                },
                {
                  heading: 'EVA and TPU: the two workhorses',
                  body: 'For roughly four decades the default midsole material was ETHYLENE-VINYL ACETATE (EVA), a lightweight, cheap, moldable foam that cushions well but has two weaknesses: it takes a compression set (it permanently flattens and loses ~15-30% of its cushioning over a few hundred kilometers as cell walls fatigue), and its energy return is modest — a large fraction of the energy you put into compressing it is lost as heat rather than returned. THERMOPLASTIC POLYURETHANE (TPU) is the other workhorse: adidas Boost (launched 2013) fused thousands of expanded TPU beads (e-TPU) into a midsole that returns notably more energy than EVA and resists compression set far better, at the cost of being denser and heavier. So EVA trades durability and return for lightness and price; TPU trades weight for durability and return.',
                },
                {
                  heading: 'PEBA and the supercritical-foaming revolution',
                  body: 'The current high-performance foam is PEBA (polyether block amide, sold as Pebax), a nylon-family elastomer that is both very light and springs back with unusually high energy return. The other half of the revolution is not a new polymer but a new WAY of foaming: SUPERCRITICAL fluid foaming, where the polymer is saturated with CO₂ or nitrogen held at supercritical conditions (above its critical temperature and pressure, so it behaves as neither pure liquid nor gas) and then rapidly depressurized, nucleating a huge number of extremely fine, uniform cells. The result is a foam that is simultaneously lower-density, softer, and higher-returning than the same polymer foamed conventionally. Nike\'s ZoomX (a supercritical PEBA-based foam) is the best-known example, and it underlies the marathon "super-shoe" era from the Vaporfly 4% (2017) onward. The judge-relevant point: "PEBA" names the polymer, "supercritical" names the process, and a project should not conflate the two.',
                },
              ],
              workedExample: 'A student weighs equal-volume midsole samples cut to the same 30 cm³ from three shoes and measures: EVA sample = 6.0 g (density 0.20 g/cm³), Boost e-TPU = 8.4 g (0.28 g/cm³), ZoomX supercritical PEBA = 3.3 g (0.11 g/cm³). They then run a drop-test to estimate energy return (drop-height rebound as a percent of drop height): EVA returns ~65%, Boost ~76%, ZoomX ~85%. The student reports the correct two-part finding: the PEBA foam is both the LIGHTEST (lowest density) AND the highest-returning, which is exactly why supercritical PEBA displaced denser TPU at the elite level — it broke the old assumption that lighter foam must cushion or return worse. They are careful to attribute the density-plus-return gain to the supercritical process, not to "PEBA is magic."',
              judgeFraming: 'Judges ask a footwear student to name the actual material and process because "it has a special foam" is marketing, not mechanics. A student who can say "it is a supercritical-foamed PEBA — the polymer gives high energy return and the supercritical process gives low density and fine cells" is demonstrating they understand that a midsole\'s behavior traces to specific, measurable material and manufacturing choices, and that density and energy return are separable properties they can actually measure with a scale and a drop-test.',
