              readingMinutes: 6,
              sections: [
                {
                  heading: 'Real surfaces span a wide stiffness range',
                  body: 'The surfaces a student can actually test span orders of magnitude in stiffness. CONCRETE and ASPHALT are extremely stiff. A synthetic TARTAN TRACK is engineered to be moderately compliant AND to return energy elastically — a good track balances shock reduction against energy return for performance. GRASS is compliant but variable (soft when wet, firmer when dry, uneven from tufts and dips). A TREADMILL belt on a sprung deck is typically more compliant than an outdoor road. SAND is at the far compliant end and is unusual because it deforms plastically (it does not spring back) rather than elastically. Naming where a surface sits on this range is the first step in predicting its effect.',
                },
                {
                  heading: 'Sand: high cost, low return, changed mechanics',
                  body: 'Sand deserves special attention because it behaves unlike the others. Running or walking on soft dry sand costs far more energy than on a firm surface — a classic study by Lejeune, Willems and Heglund (1998) measured that walking on sand costs about 2.1-2.7 times as much metabolic energy as walking on a hard surface (and requires roughly 1.6-2.5 times as much mechanical work), while running on sand costs about 1.6 times as much energy. The reasons are twofold: sand absorbs mechanical energy that a hard surface would return (it deforms plastically and does not recoil), and it reduces the efficiency of muscle-tendon energy recovery. Mechanically, people also shorten their stride and change foot placement to cope with the unstable, yielding surface. So sand is not just "softer grass" — it is a qualitatively different, energy-dissipating surface.',
                },
                {
                  heading: 'The treadmill-vs-overground question',
                  body: 'A practical debate for any project: is treadmill running the same as overground? The kinematics are broadly similar, but there are real, documented differences. The belt\'s compliant sprung deck usually lowers impact somewhat versus a road, the constant belt speed removes the small speed fluctuations of overground running, and there is no air resistance (which is why researchers sometimes add a ~1% treadmill incline to approximate the energy cost of outdoor running, following Jones and Doust, 1996). A student comparing a shoe on a treadmill and drawing conclusions about road running must acknowledge the surface and speed-control differences, not treat the two as interchangeable.',
                },
              ],
              workedExample: 'A student compares walking energy cost on a firm gym floor versus soft dry sand using heart rate and rating-of-perceived-exertion as a metabolic proxy at matched walking speed. They find perceived exertion and heart rate rise sharply on sand — consistent with the Lejeune et al. (1998) finding of roughly 2.1-2.7x higher metabolic energy cost for walking on sand. Crucially, they DON\'T attribute this to "more impact"; they correctly explain sand deforms plastically and absorbs energy that a firm surface would return, and note that subjects shortened their stride on sand. By separating "energy cost" from "impact force," they avoid the common error of assuming a softer surface must mean an easier, gentler run.',
              judgeFraming: 'Judges ask surface-comparison students to distinguish IMPACT reduction from ENERGY cost, because the two move in opposite directions on very soft ground: sand lowers peak impact yet dramatically raises metabolic cost. A student who can cite the roughly 2.1-2.7x metabolic energy cost of sand walking (Lejeune et al., 1998) and explain it as plastic, non-returning deformation — not as extra impact — shows they understand that "softer" is not a single axis but at least two separate consequences.',
