              sections: [
                {
                  heading: 'Three kinds of variability, kept separate',
                  body: 'Footwear data contain at least three distinct spreads that beginners conflate. WITHIN-subject trial-to-trial variability is how much one person\'s value wobbles across repeat trials of the SAME condition (your measurement noise). BETWEEN-subject variability is how much people differ from each other. And the INDIVIDUAL RESPONSE is whether a given person got better or worse under the intervention. These are not interchangeable: you need within-subject repeatability to know your noise floor, between-subject spread to describe your sample, and individual responses to know whether a group mean is hiding disagreement. A project that reports only a group mean ± SD has collapsed all three into one number and lost the story.',
                },
                {
                  heading: 'The mean can lie: opposite individual responses',
                  body: 'A defining, well-documented feature of footwear/surface biomechanics is that the "same" shoe can LOWER one runner\'s loading rate and RAISE another\'s — because runners differ in habitual strike, leg stiffness, and how they adapt. The consequence is that a group MEAN change near zero can be the average of large opposite responses, not "no effect for anyone." This is why footwear recommendations are so often individual, and why a responsible write-up plots the individual before-after lines (a "spaghetti plot") rather than only the mean. If half your subjects improved and half worsened, the honest headline is "response was individual," not "no effect."',
                },
                {
                  heading: 'Effect size vs. statistical significance',
                  body: 'A p-value answers only "is this difference unlikely to be pure chance?" — it says NOTHING about whether the difference is big enough to matter. With enough subjects, a mechanically trivial 2% loading-rate change can reach p < 0.05. Reporting an EFFECT SIZE alongside significance fixes this: the raw magnitude of change (e.g. "−18% in loading rate"), or a standardized measure like Cohen\'s d (roughly the difference in means divided by the pooled standard deviation, where ~0.2 is small, ~0.5 medium, ~0.8 large). "Significant, d = 0.15" and "significant, d = 0.9" are wildly different claims wearing the same p-value. This mirrors the broader reproducibility-era shift away from significance-only reporting.',
                },
                {
                  heading: 'Meaningful change vs. measurement noise',
                  body: 'The final gate before crediting a footwear effect: is the difference larger than your own measurement\'s repeatability? If a subject\'s loading rate varies by ±6% just repeating the SAME shoe on different days, then a 4% difference between two shoes sits inside that noise and cannot be called a real effect. The formal version of this is the MINIMAL DETECTABLE CHANGE (MDC) — computed from the measurement\'s test-retest error — below which a difference is indistinguishable from noise. Establishing repeatability by re-running a condition, and reporting the MDC, is what separates reading signal from reading noise, and it is among the first things a sharp judge will probe.',
                },
              ],
              workedExample: 'A student finds shoe B lowers group-mean loading rate by 4% versus shoe A, p = 0.03, Cohen\'s d = 0.2 (small). Before celebrating, they check repeatability: re-running shoe A on separate days, each subject varied by about ±6%, giving a minimal detectable change larger than 4%. They also plot individual responses and see 4 of 12 subjects got WORSE in shoe B. Their conclusion: "The mean reduction is small (d ≈ 0.2), sits within our ±6% measurement noise, and hides opposite individual responses in a third of subjects; we cannot claim shoe B is universally better, only that most — not all — of our sample showed a small reduction." This is far stronger than "shoe B is better, p < 0.05."',
              judgeFraming: 'Judges probe variability and effect size because "p < 0.05" is the most over-trusted phrase at a science fair, and footwear data are precisely where a significant group mean can hide opposite individual responses and sit inside the measurement noise. A student who separates within-subject noise from between-subject spread from individual response, reports an effect size (not just significance), plots the individual responses, and compares the effect against their minimal detectable change is demonstrating the statistical maturity that distinguishes a rigorous project from a plausible-looking one.',
