                  heading: 'The instrument problem for a real classroom project',
                  body: 'A laboratory force plate (Kistler, AMTI, Bertec) costs tens of thousands of dollars, which puts true posturography out of reach for most classrooms. Two low-cost proxies have been validated well enough to make credible student projects possible, and knowing WHICH signal each one actually provides — and how well it agrees with a lab plate — is the difference between a defensible project and a gadget demo. The two are (1) the accelerometer/gyroscope in a smartphone strapped near the body\'s center of mass (typically at the lower back / L5-S1 or sternum), and (2) the Nintendo Wii Balance Board (WBB), a consumer device with four pressure transducers that, like a real plate, can compute a center of pressure.',
