              sections: [
                {
                  heading: 'A different question: not "where is the COM" but "is the body rotating?"',
                  body: 'The XCoM and margin of stability ask a translational question — where is the COM heading relative to the base. WHOLE-BODY ANGULAR MOMENTUM (WBAM) asks a rotational one — is the whole body about to tip or spin about its own COM? WBAM, denoted H, is the sum over every body segment of that segment\'s angular momentum about the body\'s COM, combining each segment\'s spin and its motion around the COM. It is usually normalized by body mass, height, and walking speed so subjects can be compared, and reported as a dimensionless quantity across the gait cycle. The central empirical finding, from Herr and Popovic ("Angular momentum in human walking," Journal of Experimental Biology, 2008), is that despite large angular momenta in individual limbs, whole-body angular momentum in normal walking is regulated to remain remarkably SMALL and tightly bounded near zero throughout the stride — the swinging arms and legs actively cancel one another. A body that keeps H near zero is a body that is not tipping.',
                },
                {
                  heading: 'Why WBAM catches instability that COM measures can miss',
                  body: 'WBAM is COMPLEMENTARY to the XCoM/MoS framework rather than redundant. The margin of stability treats the body as a point mass (the COM) relative to the base; WBAM captures rotational disturbances the point-mass picture cannot — for example a trunk lurch, an arm flail, or a segmental configuration that is rotating the body even while the COM itself is still inside the base. Neptune and colleagues (Silverman & Neptune, and Neptune & McGowan) showed how specific muscles and the arm swing regulate WBAM to keep it bounded, and that populations with impaired balance — amputees, stroke survivors, and older adults — show a WIDER RANGE of whole-body angular momentum across the stride, i.e., poorer regulation of body rotation. A larger WBAM range means the body is letting rotational disturbances grow, an early mechanical sign of instability that translational COM-in-base measures alone may not flag.',
                },
                {
                  heading: 'Using WBAM alongside the margin of stability',
                  body: 'In practice the two measures are strongest together. A well-controlled walker keeps BOTH a healthy margin of stability (COM not escaping the base) AND a small, tightly bounded whole-body angular momentum range (body not rotating out of control). A fall-prone individual may show a degraded margin of stability, an inflated WBAM range, or both — and the two can dissociate, which is exactly why measuring both is more informative than either alone. Arm swing is the classic example a judge will probe: restricting the arms increases the whole-body angular-momentum range (the legs\' angular momentum is no longer cancelled by the arms) and measurably degrades stability, a clean demonstration that arm swing is not decorative but a genuine angular-momentum regulation strategy. WBAM thus closes the unit by showing that dynamic stability has a rotational dimension the COM-and-base story, on its own, cannot fully capture.',
                },
              ],
              workedExample: 'A student estimates whole-body angular momentum in the frontal (mediolateral-tipping) plane from a multi-segment motion-capture model, normalizes H by body mass × height × walking speed, and reports its peak-to-peak RANGE across the stride. Walking normally with free arm swing, a healthy subject shows a small normalized WBAM range. The student then has the same subject walk with arms folded and crossed: the normalized WBAM range increases noticeably, because the legs\' angular momentum is no longer being cancelled by the counter-rotating arms. They pair this with a margin-of-stability measurement and note that the arm-restricted condition also trimmed the mediolateral MoS. The student concludes — correctly and with two independent measures agreeing — that arm swing is an active angular-momentum regulation strategy, and that WBAM captures a rotational instability the COM-in-base picture alone would under-report.',
              judgeFraming: 'Judges ask a stability project "does your measure capture rotation, or only where the COM is?" because a student who relies solely on the margin of stability is blind to trunk lurches, arm flails, and segmental rotations that the point-mass COM picture cannot see. A student who adds whole-body angular momentum — explaining that normal walking regulates H to stay small and bounded (Herr & Popovic, 2008), that impaired populations show a wider WBAM range, and that arm swing is a genuine angular-momentum regulator — shows they grasp dynamic stability as having both a translational AND a rotational dimension. Judges especially like the arm-swing manipulation because it is a cheap, decisive test that makes the rotational mechanism visible.',
