              workedExample: 'A student has a single channel: the anterior-posterior trunk acceleration of a subject walking on a treadmill, sampled at 100 Hz for 200 strides. To reconstruct the attractor they first compute the average mutual information between the signal and delayed copies of itself; the curve dips to a first minimum at a delay of about 30 samples (0.30 s), so they set tau = 30. Then they run false-nearest-neighbours, which drops toward zero by m = 5, so they adopt m = 5 (also matching the Dingwell-Cusumano convention for cross-subject comparability). Their reconstructed state at each instant is the 5-tuple [a(t), a(t+30), a(t+60), a(t+90), a(t+120)]. Plotting the first three coordinates, the once-flat wiggly line becomes a fuzzy repeating loop — the walking attractor — with each near-loop a stride. They now have a geometric object on which Lyapunov and Floquet analysis can operate, all from one accelerometer channel.',
              judgeFraming: 'Judges ask "how did you get a state space from one sensor?" because delay embedding is the step most students hand-wave, and getting tau or m wrong silently corrupts every downstream stability number. A strong answer names Takens\' theorem as the licence, explains that delayed copies of one signal stand in for unmeasured states, and justifies the specific tau (first minimum of average mutual information) and m (false nearest neighbours, or the fixed m = 5 convention for comparability) rather than picking them arbitrarily. A judge who hears "I just used the raw signal" knows the later Lyapunov exponent is uninterpretable.',
