                    prompt: 'The core of the "treadmill debate" that Dingwell & Cusumano opened is that:',
                    options: [
                      { id: 'a', text: 'Treadmills are cheaper than overground data collection' },
                      { id: 'b', text: 'The treadmill\'s imposed belt speed and sensory/mechanical constraints alter the very neuromuscular control (variability and stability) being studied, so results may not transfer to real walking' },
                      { id: 'c', text: 'Treadmills can only be used by athletes' },
                      { id: 'd', text: 'Overground walking is unstable by definition' },
                    ],
                    answerId: 'b',
                    explanations: {
                      a: 'Cost is not the scientific concern; the debate is about whether the treadmill distorts the measured control.',
                      b: 'Correct — a moving belt imposes near-constant speed and subtle constraints that change variability and local dynamic stability, so treadmill results may not generalize to overground/real-world walking.',
                      c: 'Treadmills are used across populations; the debate is methodological, not about who can use them.',
                      d: 'Overground walking is not "unstable by definition"; both surfaces produce stable gait, but with measurably different dynamics.',
                    },
