                      b: 'Correct — an accelerometer transduces specific force (non-gravitational force per unit mass). In free-fall no such force acts on the proof mass, so it reads ~0 g. A resting sensor, by contrast, reads ~1 g because the surface pushes up on it.',
                      c: 'Degrees per second is a gyroscope (angular-velocity) unit, and 9.81 is the m/s² magnitude of g — this option mixes up sensor types and units.',
                      d: 'The reading does not scale with fall speed; specific force in ideal free-fall is ~0 regardless of how fast the object is already moving.',
