              workedExample: 'A student straps an IMU to the shank and logs a 60-second standing-then-walking trial. Using ONLY the integrated gyroscope, the shank-tilt estimate looks perfect for the first few seconds but by 60 s has crept 4 degrees away from vertical even though the leg returned to the identical pose — pure linear drift. Using ONLY the accelerometer tilt, there is zero long-term drift, but during the walking segment the trace is jagged with several-degree spikes on every heel strike because 3–4 g impacts masquerade as tilt. The student then applies a first-order complementary filter (high-pass the gyro at a sub-second crossover — roughly 0.3 Hz for the tau derived in the next lesson, low-pass the accelerometer): the fused trace holds vertical across the full minute AND stays smooth through the impacts. They plot all three traces on one axis and annotate: "gyro = smooth but drifts, accel = stable but noisy, fusion = both." That single figure is the thesis of the whole branch.',
              judgeFraming: 'Judges probe "why did you fuse the sensors instead of just using the gyroscope?" because it separates students who copied a fusion library from students who understand WHY it is needed. The right answer names the complementary error profiles explicitly: the gyro drifts (low-frequency error) while the accelerometer is noisy during movement (high-frequency error), and fusion assigns each sensor the frequency band where it is trustworthy. A student who can point at their own three-trace figure and say which error each sensor contributes demonstrates they understand the mechanism rather than the API.',
